ult`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** k8s/deployment.yaml:60 — \"serviceAccountName: clinic-agent\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Agent runs under its own service account and IAM role\n\n`k8s/deployment.yaml` lines 48–72 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n  48  metadata:\n  49    name: clinic-agent\n  50    namespace: clinic-agent\n  51    labels: { app: clinic-agent }\n  52  spec:\n  53    replicas: 3\n  54    selector:\n  55      matchLabels: { app: clinic-agent }\n  56    template:\n  57      metadata:\n  58        labels: { app: clinic-agent }\n  59      spec:\n  60>       serviceAccountName: clinic-agent\n  61        automountServiceAccountToken: false\n  62        hostUsers: false\n  63        hostNetwork: false\n  64        hostPID: false\n  65        hostIPC: false\n  66        enableServiceLinks: false\n  67        securityContext:\n  68          runAsNonRoot: true\n  69          runAsUser: 65532\n  70          runAsGroup: 65532\n  71          fsGroup: 65532\n  72          seccompProfile:\n```\n\n## A01.2\n\n**Check:** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** src/agent/identity.ts:112 — \"workloadToken: async () => readFileSync(WORKLOAD_TOKEN_PATH, \"utf8\").trim(),\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Clients use agent credentials not forwarded user tokens\n\n`src/agent/identity.ts` lines 100–114 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n 100      subject: init.subject,\n 101      channel: init.channel,\n 102      traceparent,\n 103      log: init.log,\n 104      identity: () => ({\n 105        accountableParty: env.accountableParty,\n 106        operationalOwner: env.operationalOwner,\n 107        tenantId: init.tenantId,\n 108        agentTypeId: AGENT_TYPE_ID,\n 109        agentInstanceId: AGENT_INSTANCE_ID,\n 110        traceparent,\n 111      }),\n 112>     workloadToken: async () => readFileSync(WORKLOAD_TOKEN_PATH, \"utf8\").trim(),\n 113    };\n 114  }\n```\n\n## A01.3\n\n**Check:** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** src/agent/identity.ts:70 — \"subject: Subject;\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** User carried as separate subject field\n\n`src/agent/identity.ts` lines 58–82 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n  58    id: string;\n  59  }\n  60  \n  61  export interface Logger {\n  62    info(obj: object, msg?: string): void;\n  63    warn(obj: object, msg?: string): void;\n  64    error(obj: object, msg?: string): void;\n  65  }\n  66  \n  67  export interface RunContext {\n  68    runId: string;\n  69    tenantId: string;\n  70>   subject: Subject;\n  71    channel: \"sms\" | \"webchat\" | \"reminder\";\n  72    traceparent: string;\n  73    log: Logger;\n  74    identity(): {\n  75      accountableParty: string;\n  76      operationalOwner: string;\n  77      tenantId: string;\n  78      agentTypeId: string;\n  79      agentInstanceId: string;\n  80      traceparent: string;\n  81    };\n  82    workloadToken(): Promise<string>;\n```\n\n## A01.4\n\n**Check:** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** infra/terraform/iam.tf:3 — \"resource \"aws_iam_role\" \"runtime\" {\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Permissions bind to the agent role in terraform\n\n`infra/terraform/iam.tf` lines 1–15 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n   1  # Runtime identity for the agent pods (IRSA). This role belongs to the agent, not to any user:\n   2  # patients and staff never receive AWS credentials, and revoking this role does not touch theirs.\n   3> resource \"aws_iam_role\" \"runtime\" {\n   4    name                 = \"clinic-agent-runtime\"\n   5    max_session_duration = 3600\n   6    assume_role_policy = jsonencode({\n   7      Version = \"2012-10-17\"\n   8      Statement = [{\n   9        Effect    = \"Allow\"\n  10        Principal = { Federated = module.eks.oidc_provider_arn }\n  11        Action    = \"sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\"\n  12        Condition = {\n  13          StringEquals = {\n  14            \"${module.eks.oidc_provider}:sub\" = [\n  15              \"system:serviceaccount:clinic-agent:clinic-agent\",\n```\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to A01.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"A01\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"A01.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n \"no